.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1227 AM PDT Wed Oct 28 2026
(Today and tonight)

Satellite imagery shows lingering high clouds over Monterey and
San Benito Counties with patches of stratus along parts of the
higher elevations. Some stratus expansion is possible through the
morning with some clearing after sunrise, but high level clouds
will also arrive this morning and persist into the evening hours
and beyond.

Conditions remain cool and dry as large scale troughing persists and
expands, with a cut-off low over British Columbia and a decaying low
crossing over from the Central Pacific merging to help dig the
trough axis deeper into California. Morning lows hover in the upper
30s to the middle 40s across the inland valleys, into the middle 30s
for the coldest spots in the eastern Santa Clara and interior
Central Coast mountains, and the upper 40s to lower 50s near the
coasts. Highs will remain cooler than the seasonal averages with
high temperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s inland, the middle
60s to near 70 along the Bayshore, and the upper 50s to lower 60s at
the immediate coast. Winds will remain relatively light with a
gentle onshore push expected in the afternoon and evening hours.
Wind gusts might reach 20 mph along favored coastal areas and
through favored gaps and passes.

A combination of high astronomical tides and surge from thermal
expansion effects brings the risk of minor coastal flooding along
the Bayshore at the San Pablo, San Francisco, and Monterey Bays, and
the Pacific coast, at high tide today. At the San Francisco tidal
gauge, high tide is expected to be 1.9 ft above normal (7.7 ft MLLW)
at 12:56 PM Wednesday 10/28, including around 1.2 ft of surge
residual partially attributed to impacts from El Nino.

