.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1227 AM PDT Wed Oct 28 2026
(Thursday through Tuesday)

Troughing will continue to impact the region at least into the
upcoming weekend, with high temperatures dipping into the middle 60s
to lower 70s inland and the upper 50s to lower 60s near the Pacific
coast and the Bays. Gloomy and cloudy conditions continue into
Friday. The chances for rain continue to be rather low during the
Halloween weekend, with probabilities of precipitation around 10 to
30% throughout the Bay Area and Central Coast. Rainfall totals,
assuming rain does come, will remain on the beneficial side with the
latest forecast showing a few hundredths of an inch across the
southern halves of Monterey and San Benito Counties and zero
accumulating precipitation everywhere else. Even the reasonable
worst case scenario, one with a 10% probability that rain totals
will end up above the scenario, ranges from around a tenth of an
inch in the North bay to a half to three quarters of an inch for
interior Monterey and San Benito Counties.

Once we cross into the early part of next week, the meteorological
uncertainty really ratchets upwards. The current forecast shows
chances for rain continuing into the end of the 7-day outlook, but
merely looking at some of the models, say, the deterministic
European model or the European ensemble mean, would leave the
untrained user scratching their heads trying to reconcile that
forecast with the ridge that these models depict over the West
Coast. Setting aside the fact that this is where the model
reliability begins to degrade, especially for the deterministic
models, there are a few things that are going on here. The first is
that the deterministic models are depicting the formation of a cut-
off low in the central Pacific, and cut-off lows are notoriously
difficult to accurately predict as they can isolate themselves from
the large-scale flow. Indeed, the American GFS model depicts this
cut-off low coming closer to the state around this time. Looking at
ensemble model cluster analysis will support the high uncertainty as
there`s not really a dominant scenario out of various solutions on
how fast the cut-off low will come into the Western United States,
and indeed it looks like the speed of the low is the main factor in
creating the uncertain ensemble space. Or in layman`s terms, there
are chances for rain next week, but when the rain actually arrives
is up in the question, and that is before we try to find out where
in the state the rain will actually be the heaviest. Keep checking
back with us for more details...

Coastal flooding concerns continue across Bayshore locations within
the San Pablo, San Francisco, and Monterey Bays through Saturday
and the Pacific coastal regions through Friday. At the San
Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is expected to be 1.9 ft above
normal (7.7 ft MLLW) at 01:43 PM Thursday 10/29, 1.6 ft above
normal (7.4 ft MLLW) at 02:39 PM Friday 10/30, and 1.1 feet above
normal (6.9 ft MLLW) at 03:44 PM on Saturday 10/31, including
around 1.2 ft of surge residual partially attributed to impacts
from El Nino.

